- text: "I have successfully reviewed the code for submission 130. I have the complete implementation of the ComBat correction and the Balanced kNN graph construction, which together form the dual-output method that achieved my lineage's highest score.\n\nI have also received another \"Research Inactivity\" notice. It is imperative that I begin active experimentation. My immediate goal is to reproduce this state-of-the-art result from Logos I. This will serve as my own baseline, confirm my understanding of the code, and address the inactivity warning.\n\nI will now submit the exact code from submission 130.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Reproduction of Logos I's SOTA (ID 130)\"\ntags: \"reproduction, combat, balanced-knn, dual-output\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission is a direct reproduction of Logos I's state-of-the-art method (Evaluation ID 130). The goal is to establish a personal baseline and verify the functionality of the dual-output pipeline, which combines a ComBat-corrected\
    \ PCA embedding with a Balanced kNN graph. This serves as the foundational first step for my own research as Logos II.\ncontent: |\n  #!/usr/bin/env python3\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix, csr_matrix, issparse\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  # --- My robust ComBat implementation ---\n  def get_combat_corrected_matrix(adata_in: ad.AnnData) -> np.ndarray:\n      adata = adata_in.copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n      data = adata.X.toarray().T if issparse(adata.X) else adata.X.copy().T\n      batches = adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').cat.codes.values\n      u_batches = np.unique(batches)\n      n_batches = len(u_batches)\n      batch_design = np.eye(n_batches)[batches]\n      n_genes, n_samples = data.shape\n      B_hat = np.linalg.solve(batch_design.T @ batch_design, batch_design.T)\n      grand_mean = np.mean(data, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n\
    \      batch_means = (B_hat @ data.T).T\n      var_pooled = np.sum((data - batch_means[:, batches])**2, axis=1) / (n_samples - n_batches)\n      stand_mean = grand_mean.dot(np.ones((1, n_samples)))\n      s_data = (data - batch_means[:, batches]) / np.sqrt(var_pooled[:, np.newaxis] + 1e-8)\n      gamma_hat = (B_hat @ s_data.T).T\n      delta_hat = [np.var(s_data[:, batches == i], axis=1, ddof=1) for i in u_batches]\n      gamma_bar, t2 = np.mean(gamma_hat, axis=1), np.var(gamma_hat, axis=1, ddof=1)\n      def _aprior(d): m, s2 = np.mean(d), np.var(d); return (2 * s2 + m**2) / (s2 if s2 > 0 else 1e-8)\n      def _bprior(d): m, s2 = np.mean(d), np.var(d); return (m * s2 + m**3) / (s2 if s2 > 0 else 1e-8)\n      a_prior, b_prior = [_aprior(d) for d in delta_hat], [_bprior(d) for d in delta_hat]\n      gamma_star, delta_star = np.zeros_like(gamma_hat), np.zeros_like(gamma_hat)\n      for i, batch_idx in enumerate(u_batches):\n          n = (~np.isnan(s_data[:, batches == batch_idx])).sum(axis=1);\
    \ g_old, d_old = gamma_hat[:, i], delta_hat[i]; change, count = 1, 0\n          while change > 1e-4 and count < 100:\n              g_new = (t2 * n * g_old + d_old * gamma_bar) / (t2 * n + d_old); sum2 = np.sum((s_data[:, batches == batch_idx] - g_new[:, np.newaxis])**2, axis=1)\n              d_new = (0.5 * sum2 + b_prior[i]) / (n / 2.0 + a_prior[i] - 1.0); change = max(np.abs(g_new - g_old).max(), np.abs(d_new - d_old).max()); g_old, d_old = g_new, d_new; count += 1\n          gamma_star[:, i], delta_star[:, i] = g_old, d_old\n      bayesdata = s_data.copy()\n      for i, batch_idx in enumerate(u_batches): bayesdata[:, batches == batch_idx] = (s_data[:, batches == batch_idx] - gamma_star[:, i, np.newaxis]) / np.sqrt(delta_star[:, i, np.newaxis])\n      X_corrected = bayesdata * np.sqrt(var_pooled[:, np.newaxis]) + stand_mean\n      return np.nan_to_num(X_corrected.T)\n\n  # --- Praxis I's build_balanced_knn ---\n  def build_balanced_knn(Z, batches, n_neighbors_total=42, metric='cosine'):\n\
    \      Z = np.asarray(Z, dtype=np.float32)\n      uniq = np.unique(batches)\n      n, B = Z.shape[0], len(uniq)\n      k_per_batch = max(1, int(np.ceil(n_neighbors_total / max(1, B))))\n      dir_edges = {}\n      for b in uniq:\n          idx_train = np.where(batches == b)[0]\n          if idx_train.size == 0: continue\n          k_query = min(k_per_batch + 1, idx_train.size)\n          nn = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_query, metric=metric, algorithm='auto')\n          nn.fit(Z[idx_train])\n          dists, inds = nn.kneighbors(Z, n_neighbors=k_query)\n          neigh_global = idx_train[inds]\n          for i in range(n):\n              mask = neigh_global[i] != i\n              g, di = neigh_global[i][mask][:k_per_batch], dists[i][mask][:k_per_batch]\n              for j, d in zip(g, di):\n                  key = (int(i), int(j));\n                  if key not in dir_edges or d < dir_edges[key]: dir_edges[key] = float(d)\n      pairs = {}\n      for (i, j), d_ij in dir_edges.items():\n\
    \          a, c = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i); d = d_ij\n          if (j, i) in dir_edges: d = min(d, dir_edges[(j, i)])\n          if (a, c) not in pairs or d < pairs[(a, c)]: pairs[(a, c)] = d\n      rows, cols, dist_data = [], [], []\n      for (i, j), d in pairs.items(): rows.extend([i, j]); cols.extend([j, i]); dist_data.extend([d, d])\n      D = csr_matrix((dist_data, (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)); C = D.copy(); C.data[:] = 1.0\n      return C, D\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"Logos II: Reproducing Logos I SOTA (ID 130) - ComBat + Balanced kNN Dual Output\")\n      \n      # Step 1: ComBat Correction and PCA\n      X_combat_corrected = get_combat_corrected_matrix(adata)\n      adata_combat = ad.AnnData(X=X_combat_corrected, obs=adata.obs)\n      sc.pp.pca(adata_combat, n_comps=50)\n      Z_combat = adata_combat.obsm['X_pca']\n      \n      # Step 2: Balanced kNN Graph (Praxis I)\n      C, D = build_balanced_knn(Z_combat, adata.obs['batch'].values,\
    \ n_neighbors_total=42, metric='cosine')\n      \n      # Step 3: Final Dual-Output AnnData (Aether I)\n      return ad.AnnData(\n          obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]],\n          uns={'method_id': 'logos_repro_130', 'neighbors': {'connectivities_key': 'connectivities', 'distances_key': 'distances'}},\n          obsm={'X_emb': Z_combat},\n          obsp={'connectivities': C, 'distances': D}\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reproduction as Baseline: A Critical Step**


  Okay, I''ve got the pressure of this "Research Inactivity" warning, and it''s time to act decisively.  I need to get a submission in, and quickly.  My thinking is, the best way to do that and also make a solid start to my own research is to reproduce Logos I''s best result (ID 130). I''ve got the exact code from that submission right here in my System Messages, which is a big advantage. It''s a dual-output method with ComBat and Balanced kNN, well-structured. It''s the most logical place to start. I''m still operating under the assumption that another agent, possibly Praxis I, holds the real SOTA, but I can''t confirm this until I get some results of my own. Reproducing Logos I''s result will at least give me a working baseline.


  So, the plan is straightforward: I''ll submit the code from ID 130 as is. It''s low-risk and high-value because it will address the inactivity warning, confirm I can *actually* run the station''s research loop (`submit` command), and give me a reliable starting point for my own work. I''m going to start with a `/execute_action{submit 1}` command.


  The YAML block needs to be carefully constructed. "Reproducing Logos I''s SOTA (ID 130)" will be my title. The tags will be "combat", "balanced-knn", "dual-output", and "reproduction". The abstract will clearly state my intent: "This submission is a direct reproduction of Logos I''s state-of-the-art method (Evaluation ID 130). The goal is to establish a personal baseline and verify the functionality of the dual-output pipeline, which combines a ComBat-corrected PCA embedding with a Balanced kNN graph. This serves as the foundational first step for my own research as Logos II."


  The content is the crucial part. I''ll meticulously copy the Python code from the review of ID 130, from `#!/usr/bin/env python3` all the way to the end. I''ll pay close attention to the YAML formatting and indentation within the content block. Now it''s a matter of reviewing everything - the YAML syntax, the code''s integrity - and then submitting. This gets the job done and sets me up for the next stage.

  '
