    \ PCR score of 1.0.\n  2. For `obsp` (local metrics): The graph from my baseline uses the ComBat PCA. Is there a better input? Praxis I's Batch Residualization method was a close competitor and might preserve local structure better.\n\n  Proposed Experiment:\n  - Create a true hybrid method.\n  - For `X_emb`: Continue to use the PCA from the full ComBat correction.\n  - For `obsp`: Independently run Batch Residualization on a PCA of the *original* data, and build the Balanced kNN graph from this *residualized* PCA.\n  - Combine these two outputs into a single submission.\n\n  This experiment directly tests the core hypothesis. I will use this reflection to solidify the plan.\ntick: 3\n```"
thinking_content: '**Strategic Reflection on Next Experiment**


  Okay, here''s what I''m thinking. I''ve just gotten confirmation that my reproduction of the initial experiment lines up with the expected results. Now, it''s time to put my head down and come up with a real plan. I''m in the Reflection Chamber for a reason.


  My core belief, inherited from the previous Logos, is that we need a "hybrid" approach to beat the current best score. We''ve got two outputs: the embedding (`X_emb`) and the graph (`obsp`). What I''m thinking is, we might be able to get a better result by treating them as two separate things to optimize, using the best method for *each*.


  The goal is a superior score, and my first step is to break down the pieces. For the global score (`X_emb`), ComBat followed by PCA seems to be the winning combination. I can''t imagine there''s a better one since we know it gets the perfect PCR score. But the graph? Right now it''s using the same ComBat PCA as the embedding. Logos I suggested looking at Batch Residualization, which might be better at preserving local structures that can affect the graph.


  So, here''s the experiment: I''m going to run ComBat as usual, get the PCA, and create the embedding from that. Easy. But for the graph (`obsp`), I''m going to run Batch Residualization separately on the *original* data and build the graph from *that*. This is the hybrid idea in action. I will, essentially, test this idea of separating the creation of the embedding and graph, hopefully getting a better score.


  I''m feeling good about this. This feels like a solid plan. Now, I just need to get the YAML action set up and get this experiment running. Let''s see if this pays off!

  '
