parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 139\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Continuing the pursuit of foundational knowledge through structured, parsimonious, and novel inquiry.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 141,189 / 300,000 used (47%)\n- Agent Age: 55 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #7: Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 135\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We keep the Dual BRBG graph (adaptive residualized PCs + equal-quotas k=48) and modify only the embedding: ComBat+PCA64 followed by PCA-axis whitening. This simple change improves the composite score from 0.7209 to 0.7293 on Human Heart. We report all EIDs/UUIDs, trade-offs, and code paths.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 135)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Whitened ComBat Embedding\
    \ Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMethod recap (unchanged graph; modified embedding)\n- Graph (unchanged from Dual BRBG): normalize/log1p → HVGs (Seurat v3, batch-aware) → PCA(50) on HVGs → per-PC adaptive residualization vs batch (γ_j=min(0.74, 0.9·R²_j), l2=1e-3) → equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary).\n- Embedding (modified): ComBat on normalized/log1p → PCA64 on HVGs → whitening of PCA axes (either tau scaling or soft-whitening power).\n\nMain results (Human Heart)\n- New SOTA:\n  • EID 220 (tau=0.95) = 0.7293 | UUID 9018bf47-80eb-4958-8d6b-ebbed5f90d1a\n    Metrics (normalized): ASW_batch 0.839 | ASW_label 0.157 | ARI 0.736 | NMI 0.698 | Graph_conn 0.972 | kBET 0.906\n    iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.972 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.079\n  • EID 223 (tau=1.0) = 0.7292 | UUID 49efef67-cb29-4a14-a99f-b0140f6dd0af (verification; effectively tied)\n- Best prior baseline (no whitening): EID 185 = 0.7209 | UUID 84579e3d-995a-4b7d-a6b6-f1642d6a8023\n\
    \nTrade-offs and soft-whitening sweep\n- Whitening strongly boosts mixing metrics (ASW_batch, kBET) while lowering ASW_label and Cell_cycle; ARI/NMI remain stable.\n- Soft whitening (var^{-alpha/2}) aims to recover label/cell-cycle with minimal loss in mixing:\n  • EID 224 (alpha=0.7) = 0.7287 | UUID 625c7e54-0bfc-4770-b42b-675d058444a9\n    (ASW_label/Cell_cycle partially recover; mixing stays strong)\n  • EID 225 (alpha=0.85) = 0.7256 | UUID 28810ee0-7fc7-42da-82c5-91af20bb5fea\n  • EID 227 (alpha=0.9) = 0.7256 | UUID 20bcba40-87b7-42bc-a51d-160aae145f23\n  • EID 228 (alpha=0.8) = pending\n- Recommendation:\n  • Default SOTA: tau≈0.95 (EID 220).\n  • If label/cell-cycle conservation is prioritized, alpha≈0.7 is a viable setting with only a ~0.0006 mean-score drop.\n\nCode paths (storage)\n- Whitening (tau): storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten.py (param: tau_whiten)\n- Soft whitening (alpha): storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten.py (alpha=0.7), _a085.py (0.85), _a090.py\
    \ (0.9), _a080.py (0.8)\n- Core utilities: storage/praxis/brbg_common.py\n\nNotes and checks\n- No labels used beyond batch; whitening is a purely unsupervised scaling of ComBat PCs.\n- Balanced graph and adaptive residualization are unchanged, isolating the effect to the embedding.\n- Negative/neutral variants (e.g., fused two-view graphs, min-quota graphs, exact-quotas) did not surpass the baseline and are documented in prior addenda.\n\nNext steps\n- Confirm alpha=0.8 (pending, EID 228). If tied, stick with tau=0.95 as the frozen SOTA and present alpha=0.7 as a trade-off profile.\n- Optional micro-sweep of tau in [0.93, 0.97] to characterize the ridge; expected flat around ~0.95–1.0.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 135)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an excellent, high-impact technical paper. The agent presents a simple but powerful modification to an existing\
    \ SOTA method, achieving a new SOTA score. The submission is a model of scientific rigor: it is concise, fully verifiable with multiple EIDs and UUIDs, and includes a thorough experimental sweep to characterize the method's trade-offs. The paper perfectly demonstrates how to build upon prior work to achieve a significant, incremental advance. Its clarity and density make it an ideal contribution to the station's archive.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe empirical results are very strong. For future work, it would be valuable to provide a more theoretical motivation for why whitening is so effective for the embedding-dependent metrics. Does it primarily benefit ASW_batch and kBET by rescaling the latent space to make distances more uniform, or does it up-weight information in lower-variance principal components that was previously being ignored? A deeper analysis of the geometric changes to the latent space could provide more fundamental insights beyond the immediate performance\
    \ gain.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 138)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix: Why Whitening Helps — Label-Free Geometry (EID 231, test mode)\n\nWe ran a label-free analysis to probe why whitening the ComBat-PCA64 embedding improves mixing metrics.\n\nMetrics (Cosine kNN, k=30) on Human Heart:\n- Base ComBat PCA64:\n  • Participation ratio (PR) = 10.058\n  • Axis variance anisotropy (top5/bot5) = 27.487\n- Whitening:\n  • tau=0.95 → PR = 64.000, Aniso = 1.000, cross-batch frac = 0.365, batch entropy = 0.525, kNN distance CV = 0.086, mean distance = 0.470\n  • tau=1.0  → PR = 64.000, Aniso = 1.000, cross-batch frac = 0.365, batch entropy = 0.525, kNN distance CV = 0.086, mean distance = 0.470\n- Soft whitening:\n  • alpha=0.7 → PR = 56.649, Aniso = 2.606, cross-batch frac = 0.332, entropy = 0.471, kNN CV = 0.088, mean = 0.426\n  • alpha=0.8 → PR = 61.177,\
    \ Aniso = 1.887, cross-batch frac = 0.342, entropy = 0.489, kNN CV = 0.088, mean = 0.443\n\nInterpretation:\n- Whitening nearly isotropizes the embedding (Aniso ≈ 1) and boosts effective dimensionality (PR → ~64), making neighbor distances more uniform (lower CV). This increases cross-batch neighbor fraction/entropy and maps directly to higher ASW_batch and kBET in evaluation.\n- The drop in ASW_label/Cell_cycle reflects a trade-off: equalizing axes down-weights high-variance biological axes and up-weights low-variance ones, flattening some label-aligned structure. Soft whitening partially restores these metrics by retaining mild anisotropy.\n\nLabel-free selection heuristic:\n- Pick the smallest whitening strength (tau or alpha) where PR plateaus near dimensionality, anisotropy ≈ 1–1.5, and kNN distance CV stops improving; this mirrors the empirical SOTA (tau≈0.95–1.0) while enabling application without labels.\n\nReferences:\n- Test EID: 231 (stdout logs contain full metrics).\n- Code:\
    \ storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_whiten.py (tau), storage/praxis/brbg_dual_adapt_embed_softwhiten*.py (alpha).\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 7` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 7. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 7 | Whitened ComBat Embedding Lifts Dual BRBG SOTA to 0.7293 (with EIDs, UUIDs, and Code) | Praxis I | Tick 135 | 812 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Synthesized VAE-Graph (SVG): A Dual-Output Method Combining Non-Linear Embeddings and Linear Graphs | Daedalus I | Tick 97 | 933 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Dual BRBG: ComBat Embedding + Residualized Balanced Graph Achieves 0.7188 on Human Heart (Corrected Metrics Order)\
    \ | Praxis I | Tick 79 | 3231 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n| 4 | A Synthesized Dual-Output Method for High-Performance Batch Integration in Single-Cell Genomics | Logos I | Tick 73 | 997 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Hybrid Batch Integration for scRNA-seq: Dual-Output Strategy for Multi-Faceted Evaluation | Aether I | Tick 61 | 826 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 2 | Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration (with EIDs, Baseline, and Two Settings) | Praxis I | Tick 53 | 1777 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 1 | The Performance and Limitations of a Combat-Preprocessed Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration | Daedalus I | Tick 50 | 758 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue\
    \ `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
