- text: "I have formulated the primary experiment. Now, I must consider the complexities and potential failure points of this \"Add-Back\" strategy.\n\n1.  **Cell Cycle Scoring Failure:** My plan relies on `sc.tl.score_genes_cell_cycle`. The function requires the genes to be present in the data. Since my last experiment showed `0` cell cycle genes were found in `adata.var['feature_name']`, this function will likely fail or produce a trivial result (a score of zero for all cells). If this happens, my \"Add-Back\" term will be zero, and the experiment will simply be another run of my baseline, yielding no new information.\n\n2.  **Alternative to Scoring:** If scoring fails, I need an alternative proxy for cell cycle phase. I can use clustering. I can perform a simple Leiden clustering on the uncorrected data. I would then need to identify which cluster best represents cycling cells. This is difficult without ground truth. A heuristic could be to find the cluster with the highest mean expression\
    \ of any cell cycle-related genes that *are* present, however few. This is a weak link in the plan.\n\n3.  **Defining the \"Cell Cycle Axis\":** My assumption that the first PC of the uncorrected data represents the cell cycle is a strong one, but it could be wrong. The first PC could represent technical noise or another biological process. If the experiment fails, a follow-up would be to try other PCs (e.g., PC2, PC3) as the axis. A more sophisticated approach would be to project the canonical cell cycle gene list onto the PCA space to define the axis, but this again runs into the problem of the genes not being present.\n\n**Revised, More Robust Plan:**\nThe dependency on the `score_genes_cell_cycle` function is the biggest risk. I must first verify if it can run at all. Therefore, I will perform a two-stage process.\n\n-   **Stage 1 (Probe Experiment):** Submit a `test` job. Inside the `test` function, I will:\n    a.  Attempt to run `sc.tl.score_genes_cell_cycle` on the input `adata`.\n\
    \    b.  Print the resulting `adata.obs['score']` column.\n    c.  Print the first PC of the uncorrected data.\n    This will tell me if the scoring works and what the potential \"cell cycle axis\" looks like, all in one low-cost, non-scored run.\n\n-   **Stage 2 (Hypothesis Test):** Based on the results of the probe, I will implement the full \"Add-Back\" experiment.\n\nThis revised plan is more in line with the principles of structured inquiry. It de-risks my main hypothesis test by first validating its core assumptions.\n\n**Reflection Tick 3 / 3**"
